<Entry slug="adhd-diagnosis" image="/img/adhd-income.png">
  <h2><span class="label">The question</span>Does family income change who gets diagnosed, and who gets treated?</h2>
  <p>
    Getting an ADHD diagnosis, and then treatment, depends on access to care. With DataLearn, our team of
    three used <strong>NS-DATA</strong>, a CDC/NCHS survey of 2,966 children ages 2–17 diagnosed with ADHD or
    Tourette syndrome, and split the question three ways: the diagnostic pathway, access to treatment, and
    my angle, <em>severity and co-occurring conditions</em>.
  </p>

  <Plate src="/img/adhd-income.png" alt="Stacked bar chart: among children with ADHD, 23.1% of cases are severe in households under the poverty line versus 12.2% at 300% of it or above" fig="1" wide>
    Among diagnosed children, the share of <em>severe</em> cases nearly halves from the lowest income
    group (23.1%) to the highest (12.2%).
  </Plate>

  <Note>Does severity really differ by income, or do wealthier families get a diagnosis earlier, while symptoms are still mild?</Note>
  <h2><span class="label">What I did</span>Severity, and everything that comes with it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Broke severity down by household income, measured as a share of the federal poverty level (Fig. 1).</li>
    <li>Built a co-occurrence matrix across <mark>13 conditions</mark> (developmental delay, bipolar disorder, and others from the codebook) to see which tend to show up together with ADHD.</li>
    <li>Contributed to the team’s class-balanced logistic regression predicting which children receive treatment.</li>
  </ul>
</Entry>
